@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import {
  child driver, child port, attach command, session export, starter tools,
  prerequisites check,
}
@design/delegation-service.sigil from DelegationService import {
  delegation, session cancel, completion notice, stop notice,
}

component Kernel {
  goal {
    Keep the core of subzero small and free of any one child driver or
    parent adapter: child drivers and parent adapters plug in through Kernel,
    and
    core services reach a child driver only through it and never reach a
    parent adapter.
  }

  state {
    A *parent host* is the kind of coding agent a person works in and delegates
    from, and a *child host* is the kind of coding agent that subzero starts as
    an
    isolated child for one task. The same program can be a parent host in one
    process and a child host in another, and the two names never mean each
    other.

    The *parent model* is the model that works inside a parent host: it writes
    the requests and reads the notices and results.

    The *driver registry* maps each child host name to the one child driver
    registered for that child host. A *supported child host* is a child host
    name
    that has an entry in the driver registry.

    A *parent adapter* is an extension that connects one parent host to the
    core.
    Kernel keeps the names of the registered parent adapters only to refuse a
    second registration of a name.

    An *entry point* is the start-up code of one process, such as the command
    line, a server, an extension or a hook. It is outside the core, it is the
    code
    of a process that imports a particular child driver or parent adapter in
    order
    to register it, and it imports the ones its process needs, which may be
    none,
    and registers them with Kernel before the process takes its first request.
    Each entry point creates its own Kernel, so a Kernel holds only the
    registrations of the entry point that created it.
  }

  constraints {
    Kernel must not name, import or depend on any particular child driver or
    parent adapter. An entry point registers each of them with Kernel, and
    Kernel
    loads none of them by name. A child driver or a parent adapter that no
    entry point of a process registers is absent from that process.

    The driver registry must hold at most one child driver for a child host. A
    second
    registration for a child host must be refused with an error that names the
    child host, and
    it must not replace the first child driver.

    A parent adapter must reach the core only through the delegation of
    DelegationService and the task operations it offers, such as collect,
    cancel, session cancel, the completion notice, the stop notice and listing,
    and
    through
    AgentConfig
    for what it shows or decides, such as
    the agent file, each routable tier and the error texts,
    and through Kernel only to list the supported child hosts that it gives
    AgentConfig,
    and it must never be given a child driver. A parent adapter of one
    parent host may depend on another parent adapter of the same parent host,
    and
    never on a parent adapter of another parent host. A child driver must never
    be given a parent adapter.

    No core service may name a particular child driver or parent adapter, or
    import
    from one. A core service reaches a child driver only through the child port
    that Kernel provides for a child host, and the attach command, the
    session export and
    the starter tools and the prerequisites check that it offers.
  }

  cases {
    With the pi child driver registered for the child host pi, the
    driver registry
    holds
    pi as a supported child host, and a request for the child port of the
    child host pi returns
    the child port of that child driver.

    A request for the child port of a child host with no registered
    child driver
    returns
    an error that names that child host and lists every supported child host,
    and
    it starts no
    child run.

    A request for the prerequisites check of the child host pi returns the
    check of
    its
    child driver, or nothing when that child driver offers none, and a request
    for
    that of a child host with no registered child driver returns the same error
    as
    for a
    child port.

    A second child driver registered for the child host pi is refused with an
    error
    that
    names pi, and the first child driver stays registered.

    A parent adapter registered under a name that another parent adapter
    already
    holds is refused with an error that names that name.

    The parent adapter claude-code registered by the entry point of one process
    and the parent adapter pi registered by the entry point of another process
    are both accepted, because each Kernel holds only the names of its own
    entry point. A second Kernel created in the same process, such as after a
    reload of the pi parent host, accepts the parent adapter pi again.

    In a process whose entry point registers no child driver for the child host
    pi,
    a
    request for the child port of the child host pi returns the error for a
    child host with no
    registered child driver, although another process may support pi.
  }

  interface {
    Name the parent host that a person works in, the parent model that works
    inside it and the child host that subzero starts, so that every contract
    uses these names for the same three things.

    Register a child driver for a child host in the driver registry, refusing a
    second
    child driver for the same child host.

    Provide the child port of the child driver registered for a child host, and
    its
    attach command, its session export, its starter tools and its
    prerequisites check when that child driver offers them, and return an error
    that names the
    child host and lists every
    supported child host when no
    child driver is registered for it.

    List every supported child host, so that a caller can check a child host or
    name the
    supported child hosts in an error.

    Register a parent adapter by name for one parent host, refusing a second
    parent adapter of the same name in the same Kernel.

    Take the registrations of an entry point, which registers every
    child driver
    and parent adapter its process needs before the process takes its first
    request.
  }

  decisions {
    Child drivers and parent adapters will keep multiplying, such as a Claude
    or
    Codex child host and a pi or Codex parent host. Registering each of them
    with
    a small
    Kernel, instead of letting a core service name one, keeps the core
    unchanged
    when one is added and lets the child port and the delegation stay the only
    seams. The driver registry holds one child driver for each child host so
    that a
    lookup by the child host of an agent definition is never ambiguous.

    The second parent host, pi, needed no further use of the names of the
    registered parent adapters, so Kernel still keeps them only to refuse a
    duplicate. Parent host and child host are separate names because the same
    program, pi, is a child host for the Claude Code parent host and a
    parent host of its own.
  }
}
